q=A.ce(A.a7(["Python","def delete_value(head, value):\n    # Edge Case: The list is empty\n    if head is None: return None\n    \n    # Edge Case: Deleting the Head node\n    if head.value == value:\n        return head.next # The second node becomes the new Head\n        \n    current = head\n    prev = None\n    \n    # Traverse to find the node\n    while current and current.value != value:\n        prev = current\n        current = current.next\n        \n    # If the value wasn't found\n    if current is None: return head\n    \n    # The Pointer Bypass (Deletion)\n    prev.next = current.next\n    \n    return head\n","Java","Node deleteValue(Node head, int value) {\n    if (head == null) return null;\n    \n    if (head.value == value) {\n        return head.next;\n    }\n    \n    Node current = head;\n    Node prev = null;\n    \n    while (current != null && current.value != value) {\n        prev = current;\n        current = current.next;\n    }\n    \n    if (current == null) return head;\n    \n    prev.next = current.next;\n    return head;\n}\n","C++","Node* deleteValue(Node* head, int value) {\n    if (head == nullptr) return nullptr;\n    \n    if (head->value == value) {\n        Node* newHead = head->next;\n        delete head; // C++ requires manual memory cleanup!\n        return newHead;\n    }\n    \n    Node* current = head;\n    Node* prev = nullptr;\n    \n    while (current != nullptr && current->value != value) {\n        prev = current;\n        current = current->next;\n    }\n    \n    if (current == nullptr) return head;\n    \n    prev->next = current->next;\n    delete current; // Prevent memory leaks\n    \n    return head;\n}\n","JavaScript","function deleteValue(head, value) {\n    if (head === null) return null;\n    \n    if (head.value === value) {\n        return head.next;\n    }\n    \n    let current = head;\n    let prev = null;\n    \n    while (current !== null && current.value !== value) {\n        prev = current;\n        current = current.next;\n    }\n    \n    if (current === null) return head;\n    \n    prev.next = current.next;\n    return head;\n}\n"],g,g),o,p,"Surgically remove data without shifting a single element! Learn how to delete nodes from a Linked List by mastering the 'Pointer Bypass' technique. We will cover the necessity of the 'Previous' tracking pointer, Garbage Collection mechanics, and the dangerous Head edge case.","linkedlist-deletion",m,"Linked List Deletion",A.a(["The Missing Previous Pointer: To delete a node, you must change the pointer of the node BEFORE it. If you only track the current node, you can't go backward to fix the chain!","Head Edge Case: Deleting the first node requires completely different logic because you have to physically move the main 'Head' pointer to the second node.","Memory Leaks (C/C++): Bypassing a node removes it from the list, but in non-garbage-collected languages, you must explicitly free the memory or it remains trapped forever."],k),q,A.a([A.a0(1,"In a singly linked list, pointers only go in one direction (forward). To delete Node C, you must connect Node B to Node D. If you are standing on Node C, you can't reach back to Node B unless you explicitly saved it in a 'prev' variable.",A.a(["To reverse the linked list automatically.","Because Singly Linked Lists only have forward arrows; you cannot step backward to update the previous node's pointer once you find the target.","To calculate the total length of the list.","Because the garbage collector requires two pointers to delete a node."],k),"Why do you need to maintain a 'prev' (previous) pointer when traversing a Singly Linked List to delete a node?"),A.a0(2,"Deletion is just a bypass! By routing the 'prev.next' pointer around the target node and directly into 'current.next', the target node falls out of the chain. The Garbage Collector then automatically cleans it up.",A.a(["By calling an internal shifting function.","By replacing the node's value with 'null'.","By pointing the previous node's 'next' arrow directly to the target's 'next' node, completely bypassing the target.","By destroying the entire list and rebuilding it without the node."],k),"How is the actual deletion of a middle node achieved in a language like Java or Python?"),A.a0(2,"If the value doesn't exist, the 'current' pointer will eventually reach the end of the list and become null. A proper deletion algorithm checks for this ('if current is null: return head') and safely exits without crashing.",A.a(["The program crashes with a NullPointerException.","The list deletes the very last node by default.","The 'current' pointer becomes null, the loop ends, and the original list is returned completely unharmed.","The program enters an infinite loop."],k),"What happens if you try to delete a node that does not exist in the Linked List?"),A.a0(1,"The standard deletion logic relies on updating a 'prev' node. The Head node has no 'prev' node! Therefore, you must write a specific 'if' statement to handle it by simply advancing the Head pointer to 'head.next'.",A.a(["Because the Head node is larger in memory than other nodes.","Because there is no 'previous' node to route around; you must physically change the primary list entry point to the second node.","Because the Head node cannot be deleted by the Garbage Collector.","Because you must traverse the entire list to find the new Head."],k),"Why does deleting the 'Head' (the very first node) require special edge-case logic?")],f),"assets/mds/dsa/linked_list/linkedlist_delete.md",B.T4)
